# ======================================================================
# advanced_anomalies.spol
# Demonstrates the semantic (not textual) rule analysis:
#   - INTERNAL is understood as the RFC 1918 ranges, so it covers 192.168.x.x
#   - an IP address equals the same address written as /32
#   - correlation, generalization and redundant-preceding anomalies
#   - ICMP PORT 0, lowercase keywords, and a CIDR with host bits set
# ======================================================================

# Exception carved out of a broader rule (GENERALIZATION, intentional)
RULE BLOCK_GUEST_SSH
WHEN TCP PORT 22
IF SOURCE 192.168.50.0/24
THEN BLOCK

# Redundant: the later ALLOW_LAB_ALL covers it with the same action
RULE ALLOW_LAB_SSH
WHEN TCP PORT 22
IF SOURCE 10.20.0.0/16
THEN ALLOW

RULE ALLOW_LAB_ALL
WHEN TCP PORT ANY
IF SOURCE 10.20.0.0/16
THEN ALLOW

RULE ALLOW_INTERNAL_SSH
WHEN TCP PORT 22
IF SOURCE internal
THEN ALLOW

# SHADOWED: INTERNAL already includes 192.168.1.0/24 (host bits set -> warning)
RULE BLOCK_ADMIN_SUBNET_SSH
WHEN TCP PORT 22
IF SOURCE 192.168.1.77/24
THEN BLOCK

# Duplicate written differently: 203.0.113.5 == 203.0.113.5/32
RULE BLOCK_ATTACKER
WHEN ANY PORT ANY
IF SOURCE 203.0.113.5
THEN BLOCK

RULE BLOCK_ATTACKER_AGAIN
WHEN ANY PORT ANY
IF SOURCE 203.0.113.5/32
THEN BLOCK

# CORRELATION with the next rule: they overlap on TCP 443 from 203.0.113.0/24
RULE ALLOW_PARTNER_ALL
WHEN TCP PORT ANY
IF SOURCE 203.0.113.0/24
THEN ALLOW

RULE BLOCK_EXTERNAL_HTTPS
WHEN TCP PORT 443
IF SOURCE EXTERNAL
THEN BLOCK

# ICMP has no ports: PORT 0 means the same as PORT ANY
RULE ALLOW_PING
WHEN ICMP PORT 0
IF SOURCE INTERNAL
THEN ALLOW
